meta {
  name: 01 Quote seats
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/centers/me/subscription/quote
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "start": 5,
    "pro": 5
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.totalSeats: eq 10
  res.body.totalXaf: eq 72500
  res.body.lines[0].tier: eq START
  res.body.lines[0].unitPriceXaf: eq 4500
  res.body.lines[1].tier: eq PRO
  res.body.lines[1].unitPriceXaf: eq 10000
}

docs {
  What a mix of seats would cost. Run `Center Auth / 03 Login` first.

  ## The mix

  A center may hold any combination of the three tiers, so the request is a
  mix rather than a single number:

  | tier | XAF per seat per month |
  |---|---|
  | START | 4,500 (launch price; rises to 4,800 later) |
  | PRO | 10,000 |
  | PREMIUM | 20,000 |

  Five Start plus five Pro is 22,500 + 50,000 = 72,500 XAF. Lines come back
  cheapest first, always, so a cart does not reshuffle between two requests
  over the same mix.

  THE NUMBERS ARE TOTALS, NOT INCREMENTS. A center holding ten Start seats
  that wants five more sends `{"start": 15}`, not `{"start": 5}`. This is also
  why the student floor can be compared against them directly.

  ## Grandfathering

  Send `{"start": 10}` twice, with a price change in between, and the answer
  does not move for a center that already holds Start. The price a center
  agreed to is stamped on its `center_seats` row, so raising the constant
  later cannot rewrite it. A tier the center does NOT hold pays today's list
  price — a stamped Start price is not a discount on Pro.

  To see it: set a center's Start row to 4,000 by hand
  (`UPDATE center_seats SET unit_price_xaf = 4000 WHERE ...`) and re-quote
  `{"start": 10, "pro": 2}`. Start comes back at 4,000, Pro at 10,000.

  ## The checks that matter

  THE PRICE IS NOT AN INPUT. Every one of these must return 400
  VALIDATION_ERROR, not a quote:

  - `{"start": 10, "unitPriceXaf": 1}`
  - `{"start": 10, "amountXaf": 1}`
  - `{"start": 10, "amount": 1}`
  - `{"start": 10, "total": 1}`
  - `{"start": 10, "currency": "EUR"}`
  - `{"start": 10, "centerId": "some-other-center"}`
  - `{"seats": 10}` — the pre-tier shape. A client sending the old body must
    fail loudly rather than have it read as an empty mix and priced at zero.

  They are REFUSED, not ignored. Quietly dropping an `amount` would leave a
  client believing it had set the price.

  ## The three floors

  A mix has to clear the highest of them, and one refusal carries every number
  needed to fix it in a single attempt:

  - `{"start": 4, "pro": 5}` -> 400 SEATS_BELOW_MINIMUM with
    `requiredSeatsTotal: 10`. Nine seats however they are split.
  - with 12 Start students provisioned, `{"start": 10, "pro": 2}` -> 400
    SEATS_BELOW_STUDENT_COUNT with `requiredSeatsPerTier: {"START": 12}` AND
    `requiredSeatsTotal: 14`. Both numbers, because the tier floor and the
    total are different problems and complying with one must not reveal the
    other.
  - with 12 untiered students, `{"start": 10}` -> 400
    SEATS_BELOW_STUDENT_COUNT with `requiredSeatsTotal: 12` and no per-tier
    entry. A student in no tier still occupies a seat.

  The ten-seat minimum is a TOTAL, not per tier. Ten per tier would mean a
  school with twelve students, two of them sitting the exam, had to buy ten
  Pro seats to cover those two — 100,000 XAF for two students, and the school
  would simply decline Pro.

  ## Also worth trying

  - `{}` -> 400 SEAT_MIX_EMPTY. So does `{"start": 0, "pro": 0}`.
  - `{"start": "10"}` -> 400. A JSON body has real numbers; a string seat
    count is a client bug worth reporting rather than silently repairing.
  - `{"start": 10.5}` or `{"start": -10}` -> 400.
  - `{"premium": 20000}` -> 400 SEATS_ABOVE_MAXIMUM, with
    `maximumSeatsTotal`.
  - A stamped price above ~214,748 on a large order -> 400
    AMOUNT_ABOVE_MAXIMUM with `maximumAmountXaf`. The seat cap alone does not
    bound the total, because a stamped price can be any integer, and
    `amount_xaf` is a 32-bit column.

  ## While BLOCKED, and while the profile is unfinished

  This still works in both cases. Finding out what you owe is the first step
  of paying, and a center that cannot take that step never comes back. Block
  the center per `Subscription Access / 00 How to block and restore` and
  re-run — you still get a quote. Clear its country, city and manager phone
  and re-run — you still get a quote, while `02 Create payment` refuses.
}
